<%# ============================================================================
    SCOPE CHIPS, and the banner that gathers them -- ONE partial, ONE definition.

    #42: every control group on FLUX, BP and JEV is one of three things, and the
    page never said which. Someone tuning the JEV confidence floor could not tell
    it did nothing, because the caveat lived in a blurb's popup and in a model
    comment rather than where the field was.

    THE THREE SCOPES, and nothing else:

      "per job"            takes effect on the next job
      "service · restart"  the service reads it at start-up; a restart applies it
      "code · read-only"   a preview of a value in another repo's source

    The partial renders ONE chip per call. A group that is a mix (FLUX's Model
    card: env vars AND request parameters) takes an extra, SEPARATE chip -- the
    page's rule stays "each control group carries a scope chip", and the mix says
    so rather than being flattened to one word.

    THE CHIP is the studio's OWN tokens, never a second hand-tuned pill: the
    .chip / .tech-tag rule (#93) carries the pill's shape, and the semantic band
    suffix (.badge.pass / .badge.warn / .badge.fail, #93) carries the MEANING as
    a colour -- per-job is the healthy case (pass), service is a state (warn),
    code is read-only (fail). A scope therefore reads as a colour and not only as
    a word.

    THE BANNER is the same three chips in the same order, each with one sentence
    of copy. It is the page's answer to "what does an edit here DO", and it is
    three lines by rule: a sentence that runs three clauses long is not read.

    Locals:
      lines  (banner only) Array of [scope, html]; a nil/blank html omits the
             line, so a page with nothing dead has no line to spend on saying so.
      scope  (chip only)   one of the three words. Raises on anything else: a
             fourth word would be a scope nobody reads, which is the bug.
      label  (optional)    the group's name, passed through so a chip can be
             identified in a test and in a DOM read.

    Called as a banner:  render partial: "shared/scope_chip", locals: { lines: [...] }
    Called as a chip:    render partial: "shared/scope_chip", locals: { scope: "per job", label: "flux Model" }
   ============================================================================ %>
<%
  bands = {
    "per job" => "pass",
    "service · restart" => "warn",
    "code · read-only" => "fail"
  }

  # ONE chip, defined once, so the banner and the control groups cannot drift.
  chip = lambda do |scope|
    tone = bands[scope.to_s]
    raise ArgumentError, "unknown scope #{scope.inspect}: expected one of #{bands.keys.inspect}" unless tone
    data = { scope: scope }
    data[:scope_group] = label if local_assigns[:label]
    tag.span(scope, class: "chip badge scope-chip scope-#{tone} #{tone}", data: data)
  end
%>

<% if local_assigns[:lines] %>
  <%# THE BANNER: three lines, one per scope, each a chip and a sentence. %>
  <div class="scope-banner" data-scope-banner role="note" aria-label="What this page changes">
    <% lines.each do |scope, html| %>
      <% next if html.nil? || html.to_s.strip.empty? %>
      <p class="scope-banner-line">
        <%= chip.call(scope) %>
        <span class="scope-banner-text"><%= html %></span>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <%# A LONE CHIP on a control group. %>
  <%= chip.call(local_assigns[:scope]) %>
<% end %>
